          "parserWarnings": [],
          "explanation": "Aleurone layer: The aleurone layer is a single layer of specialized cells found at the interface of the endosperm and the seed coat.The outer covering of the endosperm separates the embryo by a proteinous layer called the aleurone layer.It is rich in proteins and plays a crucial role during seed germination by secreting enzymes such as amylase and protease.These enzymes break down starch and proteins stored in the endosperm into simpler compounds, which are then utilized by the growing embryo.The aleurone layer also contributes to the seed's nutritional value in cereal grains. Incorrect Options: Coleoptile: This is a protective sheath that covers the emerging shoot or plumule of monocot seeds during germination.Coleorhiza: The coleorhiza is a protective covering that encloses the emerging radicle (root) in monocot seeds.Integument: The integument is the protective outer layer of the ovule that eventually develops into the seed coat. Incorrect Options: The aleurone layer is a single layer of specialized cells found at the interface of the endosperm and the seed coat.The outer covering of the endosperm separates the embryo by a proteinous layer called the aleurone layer.It is rich in proteins and plays a crucial role during seed germination by secreting enzymes such as amylase and protease.These enzymes break down starch and proteins stored in the endosperm into simpler compounds, which are then utilized by the growing embryo.The aleurone layer also contributes to the seed's nutritional value in cereal grains. Coleoptile: This is a protective sheath that covers the emerging shoot or plumule of monocot seeds during germination.Coleorhiza: The coleorhiza is a protective covering that encloses the emerging radicle (root) in monocot seeds.Integument: The integument is the protective outer layer of the ovule that eventually develops into the seed coat.",
