          "parserWarnings": [],
          "explanation": "Pollen grains are preserved as fossils mainly because of the exine layer. The exine is the outermost layer of the pollen grain, made up of a durable substance called sporopollenin, which is highly resistant to decay, chemicals, and environmental conditions. This makes the exine layer an effective protective barrier, allowing pollen grains to be preserved over long periods of time as fossils. The other layers (epidermal layer, tapetum, and intine layer) do not provide the same level of protection for fossilization as the exine layer.",
